  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 Days...\n\nAnother day of summer like weather is in store for our region\nbefore we get our first taste of fall. Today, ahead of the cold\nfront, high temps will be 10 degrees above average for most of us\nwith highs in the 80s. Relative humidity will also remain quite \nlow as the prevailing wind through the low levels will still be \nwesterly (downslope). There is also a good signal for gusty winds\nthrough the daytime hours today. The strongest winds are forecast\nin the foothills / gap areas to the west of Billings where gusts \nin the 30s mph will be common this afternoon. The other area of \nhigher wind gusts will be far SE Montana as the exposed plains \nwill allow for interrupted winds gusting up to 30 mph. These \nconditions will result in elevated to critical fire weather across\nthe CWA with the focus in those regions mentioned above due to \nthe stronger winds. \n\nOur first real fall cold front will move through Thursday night\ninto early Friday morning bringing in much cooler temperatures.\nWhile much of the region will be 10 degrees above average today,\nthe highs on Friday will likely be around 5 to 10 degrees below \naverage. There is a chance for showers and thunderstorms to\naccompany the FROPA as well as linger through the day Friday,\nespecially in the western part of the CWA. We will then remain\nunder the main upper level jet stream through the weekend which\nwill allow for temps to continue to cool off as well as allow for\nseveral chances for precipitation. \n\nExact timing, coverage, and amounts of precip are still in \nquestion. While most of the models and their ensembles do have \ngood agreement around the synoptic scale pattern, the mesoscale \nfeatures are still yet to be agreed upon. Regardless of what \nthe details are, cool temps as well as rounds of showers should \nbe anticipated. With these cooler temps there is a decent chance \nof the first frost or freeze for some locations. The coldest areas\nwill likely be the foothills region from the Paradise Valley up \nthrough Judith Gap. These areas have a 40-60% chance of a frost \n(36F) Monday morning and a 20-40% chance of their first freeze. \nAcross the entire CWA, no matter how cold it is, the coolest \ntemperatures of the season so far are looking very likely this \nweekend. \n\nNaturally, the chance for precip behind a cold front in September\ninvites the question of mountain snow. The lowest snow level of \nthe weekend still has very large discrepancies. Over the mountains\nof south central Montana the NBM 10-90 percentile range Monday AM\nhas snow levels ranging between 5kft and 8.5kft. It is important \nto note that any snow accumulations, regardless of the snow level,\nwill be confined to above 9kft. This could result in some travel \nimpacts on the Beartooth Pass, primarily on Sunday. No matter what\nthe precip type is though, the combination of wet and cold \nconditions will result in hazardous backcountry conditions for the\nentire weekend.\n\nAs we go into first half of next week, there is a good signal \nthat we will remain in an active pattern. Several shortwaves could\npivot through the region bringing repeated rounds of precip as \ncooler the average temperatures hang around. WMR",
